---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout>
  <main class="min-h-screen bg-[#FAFAFA] flex flex-col">

    <!-- Navigation -->
    <nav id="navbar" class="fixed top-0 left-0 right-0 z-50 bg-[#FAFAFA] px-8 py-6 flex justify-between items-center transition-shadow duration-300">
      <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto w-full flex justify-between items-center">
        <a href="/" class="flex items-center gap-2 group">
          <div class="w-8 h-8 bg-[#2563EB] rounded-lg flex items-center justify-center group-hover:bg-[#F97316] transition-colors duration-300">
            <span class="font-mono text-white text-sm font-bold">SR</span>
          </div>
          <span class="font-mono text-[#1A1A2E] text-sm font-medium hidden md:block">sroberts.dev</span>
        </a>
        <div class="flex gap-8 text-sm font-medium text-[#6B7280]">
          <a href="/about" class="hover:text-[#1A1A2E] transition-colors">About</a>
          <a href="/projects" class="text-[#1A1A2E]">Projects</a>
          <a href="/blog" class="hover:text-[#1A1A2E] transition-colors">Blog</a>
          <a href="/#contact" class="hover:text-[#1A1A2E] transition-colors">Contact</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </nav>

    <div class="flex-grow">

      <!-- Back link + header -->
      <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-8 pt-40 pb-12">
        <a href="/projects" class="font-mono text-[#F97316] text-sm tracking-wider hover:underline">← Back to projects</a>
        <p class="font-mono text-[#F97316] text-sm mt-6 mb-2 tracking-wider">Research</p>
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-extrabold text-[#1A1A2E] mb-6 leading-tight">
          How do students perceive the effects of social media on productivity and wellbeing?
        </h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-[#6B7280] leading-relaxed">
          An independent research study surveying 380 UK university students over 86 days,
          exploring the gap between what students know about their social media habits and
          what they actually do about it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Headline stats -->
      <section class="bg-[#1A1A2E] py-16">
        <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-8 grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-8 text-center">
          <div>
            <p class="text-[#F97316] text-3xl font-extrabold">380</p>
            <p class="text-white text-sm mt-1">Survey<br/>Respondents</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-[#F97316] text-3xl font-extrabold">91%</p>
            <p class="text-white text-sm mt-1">Use driven by<br/>habit or entertainment</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-[#F97316] text-3xl font-extrabold">7:1</p>
            <p class="text-white text-sm mt-1">Demotivated vs.<br/>energised afterward</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-[#F97316] text-3xl font-extrabold">21%</p>
            <p class="text-white text-sm mt-1">Succeeded reducing<br/>use, long term</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Narrative -->
      <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-8 py-16 space-y-6 text-[#6B7280] leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          I wanted to look past the usual overarching "social media is bad for you" headlines and
          ask students directly what their experience and relationship with social media looks like.
          Rather than trying to prove a causal link, this study focused on <em>perception</em> - how students themselves
          interpret their habits, whether they think that it's harming them, and if so, whether they're
          trying to do something about it.
        </p>
        <p>
          I built a 20-question survey covering usage patterns, perceived academic impact,
          wellbeing effects and self-regulation, and ran it for 86 days across student networks.
          380 responses came back - a genuinely large sample for an independent study, 
          larger, in fact, than many of the papers I was reading throughout my literature review. 
          With a sample of this size clear patterns seemed to emerged across every section.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1A2E] pt-6">What stood out</h2>
        <p>
          The clearest finding wasn't really about time spent - it was about the gap between
          <strong>awareness and action</strong>. Three quarters of respondents had actively
          tried to cut back on their social media use at some point. That's a much higher rate
          of self-awareness than older studies in this space found. But of those who tried,
          only 1 in 5 succeeded long term. Students aren't oblivious to the problem - they're
          just not winning the fight against it, largely, it seems, because so few are using any
          tools (screen time limiters, app blockers) to help.
        </p>
        <p>
          Motivation took the biggest visible hit - 47% of students said they felt less
          motivated after using social media, against just 7% who felt more motivated. Sleep
          and stress followed similar patterns. None of this was surprising in isolation, however
          seeing it converge this consistently across 380 separate people made it feel much more meaningful.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1A2E] pt-6">Where this connects to cyber security</h2>
        <p>
          This project sits a little outside my usual technical work, it's not particularly connected to cyber security -at all- 
          however, if I were to draw an interesting parallel, it would be as folllows:
          </p>
          <p>
          <em>"The gap between knowing and doing"</em> - 75% of respondents had tried to cut back, 
          but only 21% actually stuck to it long-term. This is the exact pitfall that standard security measures/habits fall into,
          one that awareness training aims to solve:
          People already know, generally speaking, that they shouldn't click suspicious links, use weak passwords, or reuse credentials. 
          Knowing isn't the issue; The gap between knowing and doing is.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1A2E] pt-6">Honest limitations</h2>
        <p>
          Now, of course this is self-report data, not measured behaviour -
          people don't always accurately judge their own habits. The sample was also
          self-selecting, meaning students already engaged with the topic were probably
          more likely to respond, which may have skewed results toward more negative
          perceptions than a fully random sample would show. I also didn't collect
          demographic data, which limited how deeply I could break down the results.
        </p>
        <p>
          Please Feel free to read the full paper below, and feel free to contact me if you wish to discuss the topic further :
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Download CTA -->
      <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-8 pb-24">
        <div class="bg-white border border-[#E5E7EB] rounded-xl p-8 flex flex-col md:flex-row items-start md:items-center justify-between gap-4">
          <div>
            <p class="font-bold text-[#1A1A2E] mb-1">Want the full report?</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-[#6B7280]">Complete literature review, methodology, and all findings with references.</p>
          </div>
          <a href="/files/SR-social-media-productivity-study.pdf" download class="bg-[#2563EB] text-white px-6 py-3 rounded-lg font-medium hover:bg-blue-700 transition-colors whitespace-nowrap">
            Download PDF
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>

    </div>

    <!-- Footer -->
    <footer class="bg-[#1A1A2E] border-t border-white/10 py-6">
      <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-8 flex justify-between items-center">
        <span class="font-mono text-[#6B7280] text-sm">© 2026 Scott Roberts</span>
        <span class="font-mono text-[#6B7280] text-sm">sroberts.dev</span>
      </div>
    </footer>
  </main>

  <script>
    const navbar = document.getElementById('navbar');
    window.addEventListener('scroll', () => {
      if (window.scrollY > 10) {
        navbar?.classList.add('shadow-md');
      } else {
        navbar?.classList.remove('shadow-md');
      }
    });
  </script>
</Layout>
